      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я переживаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I worry.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'worry'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Не переживай так.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not worry so much.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'not' with 'so'. Then Vika responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Почему?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why?' Vika asks this with a single word meaning 'why', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Главное, что мы вместе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The main thing is that we are together.' The question word for 'what' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'together'. Vika answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это главное.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is the main thing.' Notice how 'this' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'important'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Кому-то проще потерять все.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For some it is easier to lose everything.' Max combines 'someone' with 'all' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Vika responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Зачем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What for?' Vika asks this with a single word meaning 'what for', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Чтобы понять.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In order to understand.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'so that' with 'to understand'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Понять что?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'To understand what?' Vika links 'to understand' directly with 'what' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Насколько это было дорого.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How dear it was.' Max combines 'as far as' with 'expensive' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Vika responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нам не надо терять.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We do not need to lose.' Russian expresses this impersonally, literally saying 'to me' or 'to you' with 'to lose'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
